    "title": "Kansas could lose over $10 million in SNAP funding - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Keller Welton ,\n57 minutes ago\nTOPEKA ( KSNT ) – Kansas House Speaker Daniel Hawkins put out a statement criticizing the Department for Children and Families (DCF) and Governor Kelly’s refusal to give the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) information regarding SNAP recipients.\nLast week, the USDA sent a letter to the DCF and Governor Kelly informing them that they have 30 days to comply with their eligibility data request before federal funding would be withheld. The information the USDA is requesting includes names, birthdays, social security numbers, home addresses and income information The letter states that the federal government will withhold $10.4 million in funds used for the administration of the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) for each quarter that the DCF fails to comply.\nThe Kansas band that almost made it big in the 70s “We could save losing, you know, $10 million,” Kansas GOP member Michael Austin told 27 News. “And that’s money that could be used for needy families in order to have food. It can be used just for regular Kansans. I mean, the effects are are pretty large. And so math wise, I think it just makes sense to comply instead of having this political fight and risking the whole program, failing.”\nDemocrats have argued that because the error rate for SNAP has gone down in the past few years and that the timing of the request does not add up. They claim this is a privacy issue, and it has nothing to do with fraud or waste.\nTrade war threatens Kansas soy industry, despite record yields “I don’t know any Kansan that wants the federal government to have the most intimate details of their lives at their fingertips without any kind of significant oversight,” Democratic Representative Alexis Simmons told 27 News. “And the governor understands that, and that’s what she’s advocating for.”\nGovernor Kelly has pointed to a lawsuit several states have joined challenging the overturning of data, and she doesn’t appear to be willing to give up Kansans personal information. But withholding the data means serious SNAP cuts.\nFor more local news, click here . Keep up with the latest breaking news in northeast Kansas by downloading our mobile app and by signing up for our news email alerts . Sign up for our Storm Track Weather app by clicking here .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KSNT 27 News.",
